.TH MKCARD 10.1
.SH NAME
mkcard, mkrootfs \- build a card image and a kernel root for the native kernel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tools/mkcard.py
.I image
.I megabytes
[
.BI / dest = src
|
.BI / dir =
] ...
.PP
.B tools/mkrootfs.py
.I output.c
[
.BI / dest = src
|
.BI / dir =
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
These run on the host, in Python, and need nothing else.
Between them they make the two places the native kernel finds files:
the small root compiled into its image, and the card.
.PP
Both take the same manifest.
.BI / dest = src
puts the host file
.I src
at
.IR dest ;
for
.IR mkcard ,
if
.I src
is a directory it is copied whole.
.BI / dir =
with nothing after the
.B =
makes an empty directory.
Directories above a
.I dest
are made as needed.
.SS mkcard
.I Mkcard
writes
.IR image ,
of the given size, as a card for the native kernel: a master boot record,
one FAT32 partition beginning at sector 2048, and the files named.
It is what a Raspberry Pi's card looks like to
.IR osinit (8),
and the same image boots a board, QEMU's
.B raspi3b
(as
.BR "-drive if=sd" )
and QEMU's
.B virt
(as a
.BR virtio-blk-device ).
.PP
It writes the file system itself, so it needs neither root, a loop
mount, nor
.IR mtools ,
and an image built twice from the same tree is the same bytes:
timestamps are fixed, names are taken in sorted order, and each file and
directory is laid out contiguously.
Every name that is not already an upper-case 8.3 name \- in an Inferno
tree, every name \- gets a long-name entry, which is what
.IR dossrv (4)
reads, and a generated short alias
.RB ( NAME~1.EXT )
because the format requires one.
.PP
The cluster size is the largest of 4096, 2048, 1024 and 512 bytes that
still leaves the 65525 clusters that make a volume FAT32;
the type of a FAT volume is decided by that count and by nothing written
in it.
About 40MB is the least that can be one.
.I Mkcard
says what it made:
.IP
.EX
mkcard: card.img: 256MB, FAT32, 2048-byte clusters,
    2336 files in 97 directories, 46MB used
.EE
.SS mkrootfs
.I Mkrootfs
writes
.IR output.c ,
the tables
.B /os/port/devroot.c
serves the kernel's own root from: every file compiled in as an array of
bytes.
It stands in for the
.I mkroot
of Inferno's other native kernels.
The kernel build runs it; it is rarely run by hand.
That root is a recovery floor \-
.IR osinit ,
the shell, file utilities,
.IR dossrv ,
the USB and Ethernet drivers \- and everything else comes from the card.
.SH EXAMPLE
A card holding a whole user space, that boots to the desktop with no
login:
.IP
.EX
printf 'local\en' > /tmp/rootpath;  : > /tmp/skiplogon
tools/mkcard.py card.img 256 /dis=dis /lib=lib \e
    /fonts=fonts /icons=icons /usr= \e
    /rootpath=/tmp/rootpath /skiplogon=/tmp/skiplogon
.EE
.PP
The files that control a machine from its card are described in
.IR osinit (8).
.SH SOURCE
.B /tools/mkcard.py
.br
.B /tools/mkrootfs.py
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sd (3),
.IR dossrv (4),
.IR osinit (8)
.br
.B /docs/BAREMETAL.md
.SH BUGS
.I Mkcard
only writes; it cannot add to an image or read one.
.br
QEMU's SD card model, unlike a card reader, refuses an image whose size is
not a power of two.
.br
A Raspberry Pi also needs Broadcom's boot files,
.B config.txt
and the kernel on the card;
.I mkcard
will copy them if they are named, but knows nothing about them.
.br
The whole image is built in memory.
